  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>US Central Command on September 1, 2026 announced at 12:00 ET that American forces carried out precision strikes against Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps targets at five locations in southern Iran — Bandar Abbas, Konarak, Chabahar, Qeshm, and Sirik — following IRGC attacks on a Saudi tanker and a South Korean commercial vessel in the Strait of Hormuz during the preceding morning hours. CENTCOM Captain Tim Hawkins confirmed the strikes in an official release carried by CNBC, ABC News, Stars and Stripes, and OANN; the operation targeted IRGC naval and maritime facilities including mine-laying infrastructure at Konarak and Sirik. The F8 strike-pause criterion (a continuous 14-day pause in direct US-Iran exchange) was not met — CENTCOM's Sep 1 action terminated the forecast window before the 14-day horizon, confirming a NO resolution. The limited 16.3/day USNI-tracked Hormuz transit improvement from August 28 was erased by the tanker attacks, and Lloyd's war-risk cover reinstatement remains unobserved. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Iran launched operations against US interests in the region on September 1, with explosions reported over the Jordanian port city of Aqaba; Al Jazeera Arabic and Gulf News carried reports citing IRGC communiqués referencing the CENTCOM strikes as justification. Attribution and target identity of the Aqaba events remained under assessment as of the reporting window's close — if a subsequent CENTCOM statement or two independent TIER1 sources confirm these strikes as IRGC-directed against a US military installation, F9 would satisfy its resolution criteria before EOD September 7 and require an immediate RESOLVED-YES transition. The MFA's formal diplomatic response to the CENTCOM strikes, if issued before EOD September 2, constitutes the sole remaining observable path to the F1 YES condition. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>Bloomberg on September 1 cited an IAEA internal memo titled 'Iran Nuclear Status Unknown With US War Unresolved.' The memo documented that Iran's Atomic Energy Organization chief Eslami stated on August 26 that IAEA 'cannot seek to inspect these centres until it develops specific criteria and protocols for inspecting sites targeted by military attacks.' Al Jazeera reported an IAEA communication to member states describing an 'urgent need for access,' with the next formal IAEA Board of Governors session scheduled for September 21-25. Physical inspector access to any of the six named F3 sites — Natanz FEP, Natanz PFEP, Fordow, Isfahan IFEP, Arak IR-40, and Parchin — has not been confirmed; the CENTCOM Sep 1 strikes on southern Iran add to the stated political conditions gap before any access arrangement can be formalized. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>A Haaretz poll series published August 26 recorded Yashar at 24 seats with Likud declining to 19 — the lowest Likud seat count measured by that outlet in the 2026 election cycle. A Jerusalem Post survey from August 30 produced a tighter result: Likud at approximately 23 seats, with a new right-wing party falling below the electoral threshold and thereby removing one potential post-election Netanyahu coalition partner. Eisenkot led Netanyahu by 14 percentage points (52% vs 38%) on PM suitability in the Haaretz survey. Judge applied a +4pp adjustment (p=0.39) against the Analyst's +6pp proposal on account of poll-to-poll divergence between outlets and persistent uncertainty over coalition arithmetic for an Eisenkot-led government; the anti-Netanyahu bloc across both surveys is measured at 57-60 seats, consistently short of the 61-seat formation threshold. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>The Middle East Council on Global Affairs published a paper on September 1 titled 'Diplomacy Cannot Work with Mediators Under Fire,' documenting that Qatar and Oman face structural capacity constraints as channel-holders for any US-Iran agreement while both are under direct diplomatic pressure from Washington over their roles in the conflict. The paper recorded zero active mediation sessions in the August 14 – September 1 period, and noted that neither Omani nor Qatari officials have publicly committed to a new convened session date. The structural conditions described in the paper are consistent with the F2 probability floor and do not alter the assessment. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

