  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>Iran's state media on September 1 ran consistent framing across IRNA, IRIB, Press TV, Tasnim, and Kayhan: the CENTCOM strikes were described as a violation of Iranian sovereignty and international law; IRGC operations were characterized as 'legitimate self-defense' under Article 51 of the UN Charter; and Iranian forces were reported to be 'at the highest state of readiness.' IRGC Navy Commander Rear Admiral Tangsiri held a nationally broadcast press conference providing operational figures on patrol vessel deployment and stating that Iranian forces retained full capability to disrupt Hormuz commerce 'for as long as the aggression continues.' No dissenting coverage or independent casualty-figure confirmation appeared in monitored state-media channels. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Iran's Foreign Ministry issued a statement on September 1 condemning the CENTCOM strikes and restating that 'the path to Hormuz is the path to lifting sanctions and ending US military presence in the region' — a restatement of prior negotiating positions rather than a new formal enumeration of specific reopening conditions. Araghchi's IRNA-circulated statement referenced Khamenei's internal directive distinguishing 'IRGC operational decisions from diplomatic track decisions,' with Araghchi explicitly noting that MFA would 'not issue a public menu of concessions under conditions of active bombardment.' The MFA-IRGC institutional separation remains the stated Iranian governance framework for Hormuz-related decisions — the MFA as formal diplomatic channel, IRGC as operational arbiter. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> without move</p>

      <p>Majlis convened in a closed session on September 1 to receive an IRGC briefing on Hormuz operations; no official readout was published. Iranian economic officials cited in state media described Hormuz closure as 'a temporary economic instrument' with Iranian oil exports rerouted to China and Russia via alternative overland and pipeline routes; state television cited a 65% reduction in Iranian foreign oil revenue relative to pre-conflict levels — a government-sourced figure, not independently audited. Kayhan's lead editorial called for 'permanent' Hormuz leverage rather than a transactional reopening arrangement, framing the CENTCOM strikes as confirmation of 'the Zionist-American axis' strategy. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

